  "questions": [
    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-38355",
      "knowledge_point_id": "81247553-750f-5bf7-a1c4-698ad63b5ae4",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying True Statements Regarding Left- and Right-Continuity",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008055/assets/718dd1f38f7c790ab83639f69462b372de6b7e8501c0a4d6ff678340ab4154bd.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\n- The function is left-continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is right-continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is continuous at $x=0$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false.  The function is not left-continuous at $x=0,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=3\\ne 0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=0,$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=0=f(0)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=0,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is not left-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true.",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "II and III only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "II only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
