      "worked_solution": "Let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The function is not left-continuous at $x=1,$ since the left-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=1\\ne 3=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is not right-continuous at $x=1,$ since the right-sided limit is not equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)=1\\ne 3=f(1)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. In order for the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous. However, we have already determined that the function is neither left-continuous nor right-continuous at this point.\n\nIn conclusion, none of the statements are true.",
